Assume ename uniquely identifies an employee and one Worksfor row per employee; use stable EmployeeID in real databases.
CREATE TABLE Employee(ename VARCHAR(80) PRIMARY KEY, street VARCHAR(120), city VARCHAR(80));
CREATE TABLE Company(company_name VARCHAR(100) PRIMARY KEY, city VARCHAR(80));
CREATE TABLE Worksfor(ename VARCHAR(80) PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES Employee(ename), company_name VARCHAR(100) REFERENCES Company(company_name), salary DECIMAL(12,2));
(ii) SELECT e.ename FROM Employee e JOIN Worksfor w ON e.ename=w.ename JOIN Company c ON w.company_name=c.company_name WHERE e.city=c.city;
(iii) SELECT ename FROM Worksfor WHERE salary>50000;
(iv) SELECT e.ename FROM Employee e WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Worksfor w WHERE w.ename=e.ename AND LOWER(w.company_name)='tcs');
(v) SELECT ename FROM Employee WHERE UPPER(ename) LIKE '_A%';
(vi) SELECT ename FROM Worksfor WHERE salary=(SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Worksfor WHERE salary<(SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Worksfor));
The last query retrieves all employees tied at the second-highest *distinct* salary, if one exists.
